course: dictionary
lesson: 14054
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The words around them show you which meaning is right. «We put up a marquee on the lawn for Aunt Sara's wedding.» Put up and lawn tell you a marquee is something you can put up outside, so it means a very large tent.
explain: Now read this one: «The film's name flashed in lights on the marquee above the cinema door.» Flashed in lights and cinema door are the clues. Here a marquee is a sign, not a tent. People in the USA use the word this way.
explain: Always check the clues before you choose a meaning. Ask yourself: where is it, and what is happening? Then put your meaning into the sentence in place of the word. If it makes sense, you have probably found the right one.
fact: A marquee is a very large tent, used outdoors for events like weddings, fairs and garden parties (UK, Australia, New Zealand, Ireland).
fact: In the USA and Canada, a marquee is the sign or roof over the entrance of a theatre or cinema, with the names of shows and stars in lights.
fact: In the USA, marquee can also be an adjective meaning most famous and important, as in «a marquee player».
fact: In sport, a «marquee signing» is a star player that a club buys.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «The children had their party in a marquee on the school field.» What is a marquee in this sentence? | A small room in a house | A lit-up sign over a door | *A very large tent | A type of car
exercise: «The film's name flashed in lights on the marquee above the cinema door.» What is a marquee in this sentence? | A bus stop | A big tent for a wedding | A kind of fancy hat | *A lit-up sign over the entrance
exercise: In the USA, a «marquee player» is a player who is | A player who lives in a tent | A player who helps the referee | *The most famous and important | The slowest runner on the team
test: «Mum asked us to help put up the marquee before the guests arrived.» What is a marquee? | A car park | A garden shed | A lit-up sign | *A big tent for an outdoor event
test: «Under the marquee, the actors' names glowed in bright lights.» Which meaning fits this sentence best? | A big tent for a party | A type of boat | A school bell | *A lit-up sign over a cinema entrance
exam: «The marquee at the village fête was so big that everyone fitted inside.» What does marquee mean here? | A sign with lights | *A very large tent | A small hut | A garden gate
